# Scoped from CAPEv2's own systemd/cape-rooter.service, which runs as full
# root (User=root, Group=root) unexamined. Checked what rooter.py (#314)
# actually invokes rather than assuming, and whether it could run as a
# lower-privilege user instead of full root:
#
#   * Commands: `iptables`/`iptables-save`/`iptables-restore`, `ip`
#     (link/tuntap/addr), `sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1` -- real network
#     administration, in principle coverable by CAP_NET_ADMIN + CAP_NET_RAW
#     alone, not a full root identity.
#   * BUT: rooter.py hard-checks `os.getuid() == 0` at startup and exits
#     ("This utility is supposed to be ran as root.") if not -- this checks
#     the REAL uid, not effective capabilities, so systemd's
#     AmbientCapabilities (which grants capabilities to a non-root User=
#     without changing the real uid) does NOT satisfy this check. rooter.py
#     also never drops privileges after its root-only setup (socket
#     bind+chown, sysctl) -- it runs the whole rest of its lifetime as root
#     by explicit upstream design, not an oversight.
#   * Patching that check in vendored upstream code was considered and
#     rejected: it would need re-applying across every future CAPEv2
#     update, for a component that already runs isolated (own systemd
#     unit, own socket) and only reachable by `cape.service`/`cape-web.service`
#     (both User=cape) via the group-scoped unix socket below.
#
# What IS scoped down here, without touching CAPE's own source: root stays
# root, but CapabilityBoundingSet strips most of root's other capabilities
# (CAP_SYS_ADMIN, CAP_SYS_MODULE, etc. are unavailable even though the
# process's uid is 0). NoNewPrivileges is deliberately left off `true`
# (unlike the other three units) -- iptables' own binary sometimes needs to
# exec helper tools that expect to gain capabilities via file caps, which
# NoNewPrivileges=true would block; ProtectSystem is left unset for the
# same reason (this process legitimately needs to write outside /opt/CAPEv2,
# to /tmp's socket and to the kernel's own /proc/sys network namespace).
#
# CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE IS included, found live (2026-08-07) to be a real,
# non-optional requirement, not the over-broad grant it looks like at a
# glance: rooter.py's own AF_UNIX SOCK_DGRAM protocol replies to each
# request via `server.sendto(response, addr)`, where `addr` is the
# CLIENT's (cape.service, User=cape) own bound socket path -- writing a
# reply there is a permission check against a path this process doesn't
# own, exactly what CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE governs. Without it, every single
# rooter call (even the completely harmless startup-time
# `inetsim_disable` cleanup call machinery_manager.py fires for every
# configured machine) raised an uncaught `PermissionError` on the reply
# send, which is unhandled at rooter.py's own module level and kills the
# entire process -- crash-looping cape-rooter and, because that same
# startup call blocks inside cape.service's own initialize_machinery()
# waiting for a reply that a crashed process can never send, hanging
# cape.service itself indefinitely before it ever reaches its own
# "Waiting for analysis tasks" state. Confirmed by omission first (this
# exact hang, reproduced) then by addition (hang gone, task 1 picked up
# normally) -- not assumed from reading the code alone.
#
# Group=cape (not its own private group): rooter.py's own os.chmod() on
# the unix socket it creates only grants S_IWGRP, not group-read, and
# cape.service (User=cape, Group=cape) is the client that connects to it
# -- sharing the `cape` group via `-g cape` is what makes that connection
# work without widening the socket to world-writable.
#
# ExecStart calls the `cape` user's own already-built poetry venv's
# python3 directly, not `poetry run` -- found live (2026-08-07) that
# `poetry run` as root tries to create ITS OWN fresh venv (poetry venvs
# are per-user, stored under each user's own home cache dir -- see
# IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md's "per-user-venv trap"), using root's own default
# system Python (3.14, the exact version this whole install already had to
# route around via a pyenv-built 3.12.8 for the `cape` user -- see Host
# Constraints), and failing outright on `/opt/CAPEv2` not being writable
# under this unit's own deliberately-narrow CapabilityBoundingSet (no
# CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE). Invoking the interpreter path directly sidesteps all
# of that: same interpreter cape.service/cape-web.service/
# cape-processor.service already use, no capability widening needed.
#
# CAVEAT: the venv path below includes poetry's own content-hash suffix
# (`-t2x27zRb-`), which is NOT stable across a fresh `poetry install` --
# a from-scratch reinstall (e.g. this dev server's own planned
# wipe-and-redeploy for production) WILL need this path re-verified via
# `sudo -u cape /etc/poetry/venv/bin/poetry -C /opt/CAPEv2 env info --path`
# and this line updated to match, same as the other three units don't need
# to (they invoke `poetry run` as the `cape` user itself, which resolves
# its own venv automatically).
[Unit]
Description=CAPE rooter
Documentation=https://github.com/kevoreilly/CAPEv2
Wants=network-online.target
After=syslog.target network.target

[Service]
WorkingDirectory=/opt/CAPEv2/utils/
Environment=PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
ExecStart=/home/cape/.cache/pypoetry/virtualenvs/capev2-t2x27zRb-py3.12/bin/python3 rooter.py -g cape
User=root
Group=root
CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_NET_ADMIN CAP_NET_RAW CAP_CHOWN CAP_FOWNER CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE
Restart=always
RestartSec=5m

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
